# The camera sweep: what fills camera_listings for /cameras.
#
# Blog.CameraBrowser.Poller ran Blog.CameraBrowser.poll/1 two minutes after
# Phoenix booted and then 15 minutes after each sweep ended. One sweep:
#
#   1. search  every area x every query (4 x 78) on Craigslist's JSON
#              search API, one request at a time, 600ms before each; every
#              hit in the area's sub-areas is upserted (first_seen_at,
#              last_seen_at = the sweep's start, renewed_at, queries, tags,
#              score; a post back in the search is reopened)
#   2. details up to 80 open listings without a post page, best first:
#              fetch it (600ms before each), store body, attributes, photos,
#              times, coordinates, reply link and contact hints, copy up to
#              24 photos to the bucket, score again; Craigslist says it is
#              gone -> closed
#   3. analyst up to 200 open listings with a page and no analysis: OpenAI
#              (CAMERA_ANALYST_MODEL, 4 at a time), stored as `analysis`
#   4. gone    unless most searches failed, up to 150 open listings this
#              sweep did not see: fetch each (600ms before each); gone ->
#              closed, still there -> last_seen_at
#
# Here CAM_SWEEP does the same, stepped by the Site's tick: every request
# is http_start and its answer is picked up from a later tick, so the one
# process never waits on Craigslist, the bucket or OpenAI. Only one
# request to Craigslist (or the bucket) is in flight at a time, the next
# no sooner than 600ms after the last one's answer, as Phoenix slept; the
# analyst (CAM_ANALYST) keeps at most 4 OpenAI requests in flight, as
# Task.async_stream did. Writes go through DB with the SQL the Ecto
# changesets ran: the same columns, "" and white space written as NULL
# (Ecto's empty values), updated_at moved only when something changed.
#
# What each answer means is Temper (temper/src/cameras/sweep.temper.md):
# the search URL, the location strings, the post page (Floki's reading of
# it, checked against Floki on recorded pages), the phone and email
# patterns, classify/5 and the analyst's prompt. Here are the loops, the
# JSON, the SQL and the actors.
#
# OFF unless CAMERAS_SWEEP=1. Phoenix's Poller runs until Phoenix is told
# not to (config :blog, :start_camera_browser_poller, false), and two
# sweepers would each insert, fetch and pay OpenAI for the same posts.
# Switch-over: stop Phoenix's Poller first, then set CAMERAS_SWEEP=1 on
# blimp-blog. The analyst needs OPENAI_API_KEY (CAMERA_ANALYST_MODEL,
# default gpt-5.4-mini), the photo copies S3_ACCESS_KEY and S3_SECRET_KEY
# (S3_BUCKET, default bobbby-media); without them those steps are skipped,
# as in Phoenix.

